      "title": "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & -4\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -7 & 5 & 3\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 4 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $B$ is the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $1$st *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & -4\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -7 & 5 & 3\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 4 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4\\sqrt{7})\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+3\\sqrt{13}\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0+(-9)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
